{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering effort to decriminalize drug possession ends this Sunday, replaced by legislation that reinstates criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. Under the new statute, HB4002, individuals caught with fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice between formal charges or entering a treatment pathway. This alternative involves finishing a behavioral health course and joining a deflection initiative to evade fines, though personal-use possession remains a misdemeanor carrying a potential six-month jail sentence.\n\n***\n\nThe revised law seeks to empower police to address public drug consumption more aggressively while imposing stricter punishments on sales near locations like parks. Although the legislation encourages local jurisdictions to establish treatment options that steer people away from the criminal justice system, it does not force them to do so. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor programs to their specific resources, whereas critics fear it will result in a disjointed and unfair system across the state.\n\n***\n\nPortland Police Chief Bob Day noted that the requirements for deflection are quite strict, excluding anyone with other charges, outstanding warrants, violent history, or medical instability. When an individual qualifies, officers contact a central dispatch line to verify eligibility and summon a mobile health team, which has only thirty minutes to arrive. Day emphasized that if these professionals cannot appear within that window, the individual must be taken to jail, as officers cannot simply wait around indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nJust as a person might check their **age** before making a purchase while **shopping**, the new rules demand strict adherence to timelines and criteria. In this **country**, the debate over how to handle addiction continues to spark intense discussion. One might **eat** their words after assuming the old system would last forever, yet the shift back to criminalization is now reality. The change leaves many with a bitter taste on their **tongue** regarding the future of drug policy in the region.\n\nP.P.S This summary reflects the transition from decriminalization back to a penalty-based framework in Oregon.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.0016134874}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.02773613}, {"token": " effort", "logprob": -0.7537466}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.2947497}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " ends", "logprob": -0.026067004}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.027732883}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -3.3378658e-06}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.0015525944}, {"token": " that", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.0495124}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " for", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -8.225475e-06}, {"token": " small", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -9.274913e-05}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.005649641}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00018938188}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -0.00055298593}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " new", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.00013817311}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0035188047}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -1.3659886}, {"token": "4", "